#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "47cd3aef-8196-5e95-93f9-14c5f2ec7a40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10034" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$ (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/b6c3d21c49c399c4407247101d36d938.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2$, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -2\\sin θ, y^{′}(θ) = 2\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2\\cos θ}{-2\\sin θ}; =-\\cot θ\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/3} &= -\\cot (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2a08ba95-78ee-5a32-b785-48f3a2dd037b" :question/math-academy-id "e-628" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/11712254a9347c799840b9c88b9a8ee1.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$:\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\cos θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (\\cos θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = \\cos θ - θ\\sin θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\sin θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot (\\sin θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot \\cos θ; = \\sin θ + θ\\cos θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin θ + θ\\cos θ}{\\cos θ - θ\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "daec9915-9b7d-5029-9461-385ca8125d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9948" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n$y'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/e2076809a74372e836b4592936b7585b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ))/(2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ}{\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092105 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "1c8315d1-78ec-5f4f-90f3-1f7cffff712a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9949" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad x'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= -\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= (y^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(x^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/(2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2} + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8630a673-1333-5bb2-8ece-f91be699b2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089689} {:answer/id #uuid "8f10f858-c834-5c5d-aa3e-a4c239429b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089690} {:answer/id #uuid "c91ce8bb-be48-59b1-9708-b75df0213402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ + \\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089691} {:answer/id #uuid "d21e4829-bab9-58d0-a3e7-0dbb6fc06197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ - \\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089692} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb90a7-2c33-5d7f-b8eb-9ee611058381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (\\cos θ - \\sin θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f10f858-c834-5c5d-aa3e-a4c239429b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089690} :answer-field/id #uuid "7217078f-615c-59a4-afe1-e95bb74c82a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089688}] :question/id #uuid "303301ce-b4a4-5cfd-96c8-5b9da9450521" :question/math-academy-id "q-17115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/q-17115.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\frac{e^{θ}}{π}$, shown above."}]}
